## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level synchronization and memory-order reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Ticket-based spinlock yields FIFO fairness
- Atomics: fetch_add, load, store
- Memory orders: acquire on lock, release on unlock, relaxed during waiting

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t next;
  _Atomic uint32_t serve;
} TicketLock;
void init(TicketLock* t){ atomic_store(&t->next,0); atomic_store(&t->serve,0); }
uint32_t lock(TicketLock* t){ uint32_t my = atomic_fetch_add(&t->next,1); while (atomic_load(&t->serve) != my) { _mm_pause(); } return my; }
void unlock(TicketLock* t, uint32_t my){ atomic_store_explicit(&t->serve, my+1, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for NUMA or many threads?
- What are practical drawbacks of busy-waiting and how would you verify FIFO fairness under stress?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests fundamental low-level synchronization and memory ordering concepts in a realistic micro-optimization context.

## Key Concepts

- Atomic flags and test_and_set semantics
- memory_order_acquire vs memory_order_release
- Busy-wait with cpu pause for reduced contention
- Backoff strategies to avoid cache-line thrashing

## Code Example

```javascript
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef atomic_flag spinlock_t;
void spinlock_init(spinlock_t* s) { atomic_flag_clear(s); }
void spinlock_lock(spinlock_t* s) { while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(s, memory_order_acquire)) { _mm_pause(); } }
void spinlock_unlock(spinlock_t* s) { atomic_flag_clear_explicit(s, memory_order_release); }
```

